while you were watching this what was  the moment for you I think one of the  key moments was watching them seeing God  Save the King  [Music]  we're so used to hearing them sing God  Save the Queen and when inside  Westminster Abbey they sang God Save the  King the camera went right to King  Charles III and I thought I saw him  crying today to me he looked ashen-faced  it was like the weight of all of this  Came Crashing Down you know it was 10  days of mourning that you're getting  through the paces every day all the  ceremony all the pomp all the meetings  welcoming all the world leaders today  with that beautiful service I saw in him  the weight of this moment he's talked  about feeling the weight of History I  saw it in his face in his eyes I think  another key moment was seeing Prince  George and Princess Charlotte I mean  Prince George nine years old second in  line to the throne appear his sister  princess Charlotte of course incredibly  precocious so well behaved for a nine  and seven-year-old at an hour-long  service we all wish our children could  be that way we always our children were  as well behaved as a Prince George and  and Princess Charlotte and then we  caught a moment at the very end when the  Queen's coffin went from the gun  Carriage into the hearse as she was  leaving London and we caught a moment  where princess Charlotte turned to her  brother Prince George and said it's time  to Bow we thought we could read her lips  and it was once again like she was  keeping her older brother in line and  that's the when you talk to Palace  insiders or Royal experts they talk  about that a lot of all of this Pomp and  ceremony is not just you know it's it's  about showing the continuity of the  monarchy and so I think that's why they  wanted the children there was to show  the continuity of the monarchy this has  lasted a thousand years and it's going  to continue to last  [Music]
 

